{{NZelectorateShortDesc|type=general|current=yes|region=canterbury}}
'''Ilam''' is a New Zealand parliamentary [[New Zealand electorates|electorate]]. Formed for the {{NZ election link|1996}}, it was held by [[Gerry Brownlee]] of the [[National Party (New Zealand)|National Party]] until the {{NZ election link|2020}}, when [[Sarah Pallett]] of the [[New Zealand Labour Party|Labour Party]] unseated Brownlee in an [[Upset (competition)|upset victory]].<ref>{{cite news |title=Election results 2020: Gerry Brownlee concedes his seat in Christchurch seat of Ilam after holding it for 24 years |url= https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/election-results-2020-gerry-brownlee-concedes-his-seat-in-christchurch-seat-of-ilam-after-holding-it-fo24-years/YL356M7CUKFJHCPD35GZH2IBIY/ |work=[[The New Zealand Herald]] |access-date=17 October 2020 }}</ref> The seat reverted to National when it was won by [[Hamish Campbell]] in the {{NZ election link|2023}}.

==Population centres==
{{maplink|from=New Zealand Electorates/2020/General/Ilam.map|frame=yes|text=Map of boundaries at the [[2020 New Zealand general election|2020]] and [[2023 New Zealand general election|2023]] general elections}}
Encompassing many of Christchurch's most affluent suburbs, Ilam was considered a safe National seat until the {{NZ election link|2020}}. The electorate includes the suburbs of [[Riccarton, New Zealand|Riccarton]] (north of Riccarton Road), [[Upper Riccarton]], [[Fendalton]], [[Burnside, Canterbury|Burnside]], [[Avonhead]], [[Merivale, Christchurch|Merivale]], [[Bishopdale, Christchurch|Bishopdale]], [[Ilam, New Zealand|Ilam]], [[Russley]] and [[Bryndwr]].  In 2008, the boundaries were Deans Avenue, the [[Avon River (Canterbury)|Avon River]], Bealey Avenue, Papanui Road, Harewood Road, Russley Road, Ansonby Street, Cutts Road, Yaldhurst Road and Riccarton Road.<ref name="Electorate Profile Ilam">{{cite web |title=Electorate Profile Ilam |url=http://www.parliament.nz/NR/rdonlyres/E2BE2A11-C544-424D-81CA-31C050E9D41A/130/Ilam1.pdf |publisher=Parliamentary Library |access-date=20 November 2011 |date=October 2005 |archive-date=29 September 2011 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110929111134/http://www.parliament.nz/NR/rdonlyres/E2BE2A11-C544-424D-81CA-31C050E9D41A/130/Ilam1.pdf |url-status=dead }}</ref> The 2013/14 redistribution did not alter the boundaries of the Ilam electorate.<ref>{{cite book |title=Report of the Representation Commission 2014 |url=http://www.elections.org.nz/sites/default/files/bulk-upload/documents/report_of_the_representation_commission_2014.pdf |isbn=978-0-477-10414-2 |publisher=Representation Commission |access-date=26 September 2014 |page=10 |date=4 April 2014 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20141006075102/http://www.elections.org.nz/sites/default/files/bulk-upload/documents/report_of_the_representation_commission_2014.pdf |archive-date=6 October 2014 |url-status=dead }}</ref> The 2020 redistribution, however, added Avonhead from {{NZ electorate link|Wigram}} and a large section of {{NZ electorate link|Selwyn}} around [[McLeans Island]] and [[Christchurch Airport]].<ref>{{cite web|url=https://vote.nz/assets/Boundary-Review/REPORT-OF-THE-REPRESENTATION-COMMISSION-2020.pdf |title=Report of the Representation Commission 2020 |date=17 April 2020}}</ref> The 2025 boundary review saw the electorate gain part of [[Northcote, Christchurch|Northcote]] from {{NZ electorate link|Christchurch Central}}.<ref>{{cite book |title=Report of the Representation Commission 2025 |url=https://elections.nz/assets/pagecomponent-file-files/REPORT-OF-THE-REPRESENTATION-COMMISSION-2025-WEB.pdf |isbn=978-0-473-75233-0 |publisher=Representation Commission |access-date=22 September 2025 |page=21 |date=8 August 2025 }}</ref>

==History==
The Ilam electorate was formed for the {{NZ election link|1996}}, mostly evolving from the {{NZ electorate link|Fendalton}} electorate.<ref name="Electorate Profile Ilam" />  [[Gerry Brownlee]] from the [[New Zealand National Party|National Party]] was the first elected representative and held the electorate until [[2020 New Zealand general election|2020]], when he lost the electorate to [[New Zealand Labour Party|Labour's]] [[Sarah Pallett]] in an [[Upset (competition)|upset]].<ref>{{cite web | url=https://www.stuff.co.nz/national/politics/123128975/election-2020-sarah-pallett-promises-more-inclusive-style-after-defeating-gerry-brownlee-in-ilam | title=Election 2020: Sarah Pallett promises 'more inclusive' style after defeating Gerry Brownlee in Ilam | date=18 October 2020 }}</ref>

